    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `Reactive._compute`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_compute` method is a class method in the `Reactive` class that triggers computations for a given `Reactable` object. It iterates over the intersection of the object's reactive attributes and computes, attempting to find a corresponding `compute` method for each. If a `compute` method is found, it retrieves the current value of the reactive attribute, invokes the method to compute a new value, and updates the attribute if the new value differs from the current one. The method also checks for watchers associated with the reactive attribute to notify them of the change. If a `compute` method is not found, it continues to the next attribute. The method does not return any value; it performs its operations in place. It relies on helper methods like `_check_watchers` to manage side effects such as notifying watchers of changes. The method handles missing `compute` methods gracefully by continuing to the next attribute, thus avoiding exceptions.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of a